+[[mapping-maps-and-iterables]]
+=== Mapping between maps and iterables
+
+A map can be mapped to an iterable by mapping each of its entries to an element of the target iterable. In the other
+direction, each source element is mapped to a `Map.Entry`, whose key and value are added to the target map.
+
+.Map and iterable mapping methods
+====
+[source, java, linenums]
+[subs="verbatim,attributes"]
+----
+public interface ScheduleMapper {
+
+    List<ScheduleDto> schedulesToDtos(Map<String, Schedule> schedules);
+
+    Map<String, Schedule> dtosToSchedules(List<ScheduleDto> schedules);
+
+    @Mapping(target = "day", source = "key")
+    @Mapping(target = ".", source = "value")
+    ScheduleDto entryToDto(Map.Entry<String, Schedule> entry);
+
+    @Mapping(target = "key", source = "day")
+    @Mapping(target = "value", source = "source")
+    Map.Entry<String, Schedule> dtoToEntry(ScheduleDto source);
+}
+----
+====
+
+Entry mapping methods participate in the same method selection and qualification rules as other iterable element
+mapping methods. MapStruct can also generate mappings to `Map.Entry` from the logical `key` and `value` target
+properties, as shown above. The configured null-value mapping strategy and the usual result-container selection rules
+apply to these mappings.
+
